{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, Actin/HSP70 (c.55.1.1), Hexokinase (c.55.1.3) and Glycerol kinase (c.55.1.4) are three separate families inside one superfamily, c.55.1 'Actin-like ATPase domain'. That superfamily sits in fold c.55 'Ribonuclease H-like motif', class c (alpha/beta, a/b). The same superfamily also holds other families, such as Acetokinase-like (c.55.1.2), BadF/BadG/BcrA/BcrD-like (c.55.1.5), Glucokinase (c.55.1.7) and Ppx/GppA phosphatase (c.55.1.8). Because they share a superfamily but not a family, SCOPe is saying their catalytic domains are remotely homologous. They are judged to descend from a common ancestor on the basis of structural and functional evidence (conserved ATP-binding core, two-lobe architecture, phosphoryl-transfer or ATPase chemistry), even though sequence similarity is low. Different families mean the relationship is more distant than within-family homology, which is the closer level of clear sequence or functional relatedness.

What this permits: you can infer shared ancestry and a conserved structural core. That is a common ATP-binding, phosphotransferase-like fold, so a structure from one family can inform the overall architecture, the ATP/nucleotide-site location, and the core secondary-structure framework of the others. It also supports the broad view that evolution has repurposed one ATPase scaffold for very different jobs, such as cytoskeletal polymerization (actin), chaperone activity (HSP70), and sugar or polyol phosphorylation (hexokinase, glycerol kinase).

What it does not permit: superfamily membership does not mean the same function, the same substrate, the same mechanism, or the same cellular role. Do not transfer substrate specificity, catalytic residues, regulation, oligomeric state or ligand-binding details from one family to another. Actin and HSP70 are not kinases, and hexokinase and glycerol kinase act on different substrates. It is also not evidence of close sequence-level homology, so a model built on a template from another family would be unreliable in loops, insertions and the substrate pocket. The superfamily call also does not make c.55 a single family. The fold-level RNase H-like grouping (c.55) is a weaker, purely structural link and by itself does not imply homology beyond the superfamily. For function or modelling, you need family-level or sequence-level evidence (alignment, conserved motifs, experimental data) in addition to the SCOPe placement.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08","/api/v1/search?q=c.55.1&release=2.08","/api/v1/search?q=hexokinase&release=2.08","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53066"]}]}
